7. Second Test – Detecting a Faulty Comparator
The second dataset contains several ties:
Team	Points	Goal difference
Zebras	10	5
Lions	10	5
Tigers	10	8
Wolves	8	3
Bears	8	3

The correct ranking is:
1. Tigers – highest goal difference.
2. Lions – alphabetical order.
3. Zebras – alphabetical order.
4. Bears – alphabetical order.
5. Wolves – alphabetical order.
The faulty comparator considers only points. Therefore, it ignores goal difference and team name when points are equal.

This demonstrates why all three ranking conditions must be included in the comparator.
8. Correctness Inquiry – Why Does the Comparator Satisfy the Principle?
The comparator follows a three-level comparison rule.

First condition – Points

Teams with higher points are ranked first.

Second condition – Goal difference

If two teams have equal points, their goal differences are compared. The team with the higher goal difference ranks first.

Third condition – Team name

If both points and goal difference are equal, team names are compared alphabetically.

The comparator uses lexicographical ordering: it moves to the next condition only when the previous condition produces a tie.

This ensures transitivity because each comparison condition follows a consistent ordering rule. It also returns zero when all three ranking fields are equal.

Unlike subtraction-based comparison, comparingInt() avoids integer overflow problems.
9. Understanding the Wildcard
The generic class uses:
Comparator<? super T> order;


This means the comparator can compare objects of type T or a parent type of T.

The addAll() method uses:
void addAll(List<? extends T> newItems) {
    items.addAll(newItems);
}


Here, ? extends T means the method accepts a list containing objects of type T or its subclasses.

For example, if T is Team, it can accept a List<Team> or a list of a subclass of Team.

This makes the generic component more flexible and reusable.
10. Alternative Concept and Complexity Analysis
There are two alternative approaches.
A. Using Comparable
Instead of using a separate Comparator, Team can implement Comparable<Team>.
class Team implements Comparable<Team> {

    final String name;
    final int points;
    final int goalDiff;

    @Override
    public int compareTo(Team other) {

        int result = Integer.compare(
            other.points, this.points
        );

        if (result == 0) {
            result = Integer.compare(
                other.goalDiff, this.goalDiff
            );
        }

        if (result == 0) {
            result = this.name.compareTo(other.name);
        }

        return result;
    }
}


Now the leaderboard can simply use:
List<Team> ranking = new ArrayList<>(teams);

Collections.sort(ranking);


Comparable is useful when Team has one standard ranking order. However, Comparator is better when different ranking rules are required for teams, players and colleges.
B. Using Three Stable Sorts
Another approach is to sort the list three times.
List<Team> ranking = new ArrayList<>(teams);

// First sort by name
ranking.sort(Comparator.comparing(t -> t.name));

// Second sort by goal difference
ranking.sort(
    Comparator.comparingInt(
        (Team t) -> t.goalDiff
    ).reversed()
);

// Third sort by points
ranking.sort(
    Comparator.comparingInt(
        (Team t) -> t.points
    ).reversed()
);


Java's List.sort() is stable, meaning equal elements retain their previous relative order.

Therefore, sorting from the least important condition to the most important condition produces the correct ranking.
Time and Space Complexity
Let n represent the number of teams.
Approach	Time complexity	Extra space
Comparator chain	O(n log n)	O(n)
Comparable	O(n log n)	O(n)
Three stable sorts	O(n log n)	O(n)

Explanation:
- Comparator chain requires one sorting operation.
- Comparable also requires one sorting operation.
- Three stable sorts require three sorting operations, each taking O(n log n).
- All three approaches require temporary sorting space. The leaderboard also creates a copy of the original list.
Although three sorts have the same overall Big-O complexity, they perform more sorting work in practice.
11. Final Comparison
Feature	Comparable	Comparator
Defined inside class	Yes	No
Multiple ranking rules	Less convenient	Easy
Reusability	Limited	High
Sorting method	compareTo()	compare()
Best use	Natural ordering	Custom ordering

I recommend Comparator because the same generic leaderboard can be reused for different types of ranking without modifying their original classes.
12. Conclusion
The experiment demonstrates how a generic leaderboard can be developed using Java Generics and Comparator.

The ranking component follows three conditions: higher points, higher goal difference and alphabetical team name.

The Comparator chain ensures consistent ranking and handles ties correctly. The generic Leaderboard<T> class makes the solution reusable for teams, players, colleges and quiz competitions.

Therefore, Comparator with a generic Leaderboard class is the best engineering choice because it provides flexibility, reusability and consistent sorting.
